### 3.4 Private-data rule

The following must never be committed or included in public release archives:

- ISO, DVD descriptor, extracted XEX, XEXP, RPF, BIK, save dumps, title updates, DLC packages
- generated C++ that directly reproduces proprietary game code, unless legal review explicitly approves its distribution
- Xenia logs containing local usernames or private filesystem paths without sanitization
- crash dumps or traces containing copyrighted memory pages

The repository must provide hash validation and local extraction/build instructions so users can supply their own legally obtained dump.

### 3.5 Consumer preparation and distribution model

The target public workflow is an asset-free local preparation launcher:

1. the user selects their own legally obtained supported disc dump;
2. the launcher validates the exact ISO/content identity before any extraction;
3. extraction is contained, source-read-only, and restricted to a project-owned staging root;
4. guest code generation and native compilation run locally using pinned tools;
5. success atomically publishes a fingerprinted prepared-game directory;
6. later launches reuse that directory until a repair or project/SDK update requires a new generation.

This is already the underlying developer architecture: the repository has exact dump validation, safe extraction, local non-force code generation, native compilation, launch, and integrity gates. What remains for M9 is the consumer launcher/UX, progress and actionable errors, resumable and atomic prepare/repair/update, a portable or otherwise reproducible toolchain, and clean-machine packaging tests.

The source dump must never be uploaded, modified, bundled, or deleted by prepare, repair, update, uninstall, or rollback. It need not be present on every ordinary launch once a prepared directory has been verified, but it remains user-owned and may be requested for repair/rebuild. Extracted assets, XEX content, and generated proprietary guest code remain local and untracked. A prebuilt host/runtime plus locally generated guest-module split is desirable if ReXGlue supports it, but local whole-target compilation is an acceptable first implementation and does not change the legal boundary.

