## 6. Cross-cutting engineering rules

### 6.1 Release workflow

Use this sequence without skipping steps:

1. Code
2. Review code
3. Run `ast-grep scan` when applicable
4. Validate ast-grep rules with `ast-grep test`
5. Run project tests
6. Fix all release-blocking findings
7. Bump the four-part version
8. Update all relevant documentation
9. Explicitly stage reviewed files
10. Commit
11. Push

No commit or push is permitted before review and required tests pass.

Project delivery cadence:

- Subagents may own bounded, explicitly scoped work or provide independent read-only research and review under the repository-local `AGENTS.md`. Because all agents share one worktree, edit ownership and GUI/runtime operations are serialized; the primary agent retains integration, acceptance testing, task status, commits, usage checks, milestone closure, and pushes.
- Continue autonomously until the active milestone is reviewed, closed, committed, and pushed; user input/authorization is genuinely required; or observable weekly allowance reaches **30% remaining**, per current repository-local `AGENTS.md`. Older dated 25% overrides are historical, not current policy. After each closed task, read live usage before starting another task. If usage is not exposed, never invent a value—continue to milestone closure or a real blocker and rely on the user to signal an external quota threshold.
- Active owner exception from2026-10-11 until explicitly cancelled: the30% quota stop is suspended; available allowance may be used down to zero. Owner reaffirmed this after the28%-remaining handoff. No automatic expiry; repetition of the ordinary rule is not cancellation. This overrides only the quota condition, not other gates or spending authority.
- Owner clarification2026-10-17: continue using existing credits after weekly zero, stopping at approximately5,000 credits (conservative floor5,000). Read live balance after closed tasks and before substantial new runs. No purchases, top-ups or reset redemption; all safety/review gates remain. This supersedes weekly-zero stopping until the owner changes it.
- Close one task per commit. A task commit may be created only after that task's acceptance criterion, review, and relevant tests pass.
- Do not combine unrelated completed task IDs in one commit. If two tasks are inseparable, revise the ledger and dependency rationale before implementation.
- Recommended task commit subject: `task(Mx-nnn): concise outcome`.
- Task commits remain local during an open milestone unless an explicit backup/collaboration push is approved.
- After the final task needed by a milestone passes, perform the mandatory milestone review, update its checkpoint and gate decision, create a separate closure commit, then push all milestone commits.
- After a milestone closes and pushes with a `GO` decision, start the next milestone autonomously when no user action is required and the usage threshold permits. If closure or the next milestone requires a manual game route, visual judgment, authorization, source material, or another user-only action, stop and provide an exact checklist instead of guessing or bypassing the gate.
- Recommended closure commit subject: `milestone(Mx): close <milestone name>`.
- A milestone is not closed merely because its implementation tasks are committed; the closure commit and successful push are required.
- The repository bootstrap is the only ordering exception: Git must be initialized before the first task commit can exist. The first commit records M0-001; M0-002 is closed by a subsequent ledger/status commit after repository state is verified.

### 6.2 Versioning

Public versions use `major.minor.patch.hotfix`.

- Major: intentionally incompatible change
- Minor: new functionality or newly supported milestone capability
- Patch: compatible fix, maintenance, refactor, or documentation release
- Hotfix: urgent, narrow follow-up on the current patch line

Initial recommended line:

- `0.1.0.0`: reproducible project scaffold and feasibility tooling
- `0.2.0.0`: clean codegen/native link
- `0.3.0.0`: clean native build, reliable module boot, and bounded startup diagnostics
- `0.4.0.0`: stable frontend
- `0.5.0.0`: first playable race
- `0.6.0.0`: campaign-compatible alpha
- `0.9.0.0`: release candidate line
- `1.0.0.0`: validated public release

These are planning markers, not automatic version bumps.

### 6.3 Definition of Done for every task

A task may be checked only when all applicable conditions are met:

- implementation or document exists in its intended location
- relevant code has been reviewed
- warnings and failures are understood; none are silently ignored
- tests appropriate to the risk have passed
- evidence is recorded in a sanitized log, report, screenshot, test result, or reproducible command
- README-AI and other affected documentation are updated
- no proprietary or secret material is staged

